tages where each stage's :writes feed the next stage's :reads. The proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\n#### Alternative #1 (confidence: 0.90)\nWhy this fits: This fingerprint shares the same ETL logic as the class, which is highly suitable for sequential transformation passes: first scaling the numeric components, then checking for non-linear exceptions like bake time.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nETL pipelines chain 2-4 sequential stages where each stage's :writes feed the next stage's :reads. The proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\nAssigned to domain child f52f474b-2938-36fd-883c-c4824d1074f0 (label: recipe-scaling) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 1 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Transformation (confidence: 0.85)\n   Why this fits: Scaling recipe ingredients is a deterministic, mechanical reshape of data based on a fixed ratio (30/8). The candidate's recommendation to use :code for deterministic transformations ensures byte-for-byte fidelity and avoids the hallucination risks of an LLM when performing simple multiplication. However, because the task also requires 'flagging anything that won't scale linearly' (judgment), a hybrid approach using :code for the math and :llm for the semantic analysis is the ideal implementation.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Transformation is mechanical reshape of data between deterministic shapes. ALWAYS use :code (not :llm) for deterministic transformations — preserves fidelity, costs nothing, runs in ms; :llm for transformation produces non-deterministic output. For per-item independent transformations, :map-each isolates per-item failures. ALWAYS declare :output-schemas on transformation output and :input-schemas on downstream :reads so shape mismatch fails at the boundary, not deep in the consumer. Composes into etl-pipeline (sequential transform chain), parallel-sum (map-reduce transformation), sequential-pipeline shells, and map-reduce shells. Avoid for transformations that need judgment (semantic rephrasing / style shift — use :llm) or for unstructured input (extract first).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given input data + a target shape spec, emit the input restructured to the target shape\n  - natural fit anywhere a tree needs to change data shape without semantic interpretation\n  - outputs are typically a single restructured value or a vector of per-item transformations\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** implement transformations in :code (NOT :llm) when the mapping is deterministic — preserves byte-for-byte fidelity, costs zero tokens, runs in milliseconds (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the transformation rule can be expressed as a pure function over the input\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:output] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:output (mapv (fn [r] (select-keys r [:a :b])) (:input inputs))})}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for per-item independent transformations over a vector, use :map-each rather than a single batch :code call when the per-item work is non-trivial — supports per-item failure isolation (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: items can fail independently AND partial success has value\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:map-each {:items :records :as :record :writes [:transformed]} [:code {:reads [:record] :writes [:transformed] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:transformed (assoc (:record inputs) :stamp \"x\")})}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** using :llm for a deterministic transformation (CSV → JSON, snake_case → camelCase) produces non-deterministic output AND wastes tokens (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the transformation rule is fully specifiable\n    - Recommended fix: always :code for deterministic transformations; reserve :llm for transformations that need judgment (semantic-rephrasing / style-shift / domain-translation)\n\n  - **Failure mode:** transformations without :output-schemas on the downstream :reads break silently when an upstream change shifts the output shape — the consumer gets garbage instead of an error (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the transformation feeds another stage that binds on specific fields\n    - Recommended fix: declare :output-schemas on the transformation output AND :input-schemas on the downstream :reads — shape mismatch fails loudly at the boundary, not deep in the consumer\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - CSV row → JSON object mapping per-field\n  - extracted entity records → DB-row shape with derived fields\n  - input list → output list with N filters / projections applied\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                        :summary \"<2-3 sentences naming the domain + load-bearing trait + when to prefer this over the parent>\"\n                        :version 1\n                        :consolidated-from-event-count 0}\n         